<li>Wipe cupboard fronts, handles, the bin lid and the extractor hood.</li>
</ul>
<p>Smells are harder to fix than visible dirt. Cooking odours, damp, smoke and pets linger in soft furnishings and carpets. Ventilate well during every clean, take bins outside, and if you accept pets, follow our tips on <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">dealing with pet hair and odours</a>. A periodic <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> and professional <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a> lift what everyday cleaning can't reach.</p>
<h2>Build a repeatable Airbnb cleaning system</h2>
<p>Hosts with consistently high cleanliness scores rarely rely on memory. They use the same checklist at every turnover, whoever is cleaning, so standards don't drop on busy weekends or when a regular cleaner is away.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Write it down.</strong> Start with our <a href="{{guide:holiday-let-changeover-checklist}}">holiday let changeover checklist</a> and adapt it to your property.</li>
<li><strong>Set a presentation standard.</strong> Photograph how each room should look, from towel folding to cushion placement.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for photo reports.</strong> A quick set of photos after each clean lets you check standards remotely.</li>
<li><strong>Schedule deeper tasks.</strong> Put ovens, carpets, curtains and mattresses on a calendar rather than waiting until someone notices.</li>
<li><strong>Read every review.</strong> Even a four-star cleanliness score tells you something, so look for patterns.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you are bringing in outside help, our guide to <a href="{{guide:how-to-choose-a-cleaner}}">choosing a cleaner you can trust</a> explains what to check, from insurance to DBS checks.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Stay a night in your own listing every so often, or ask a friend to. Using the shower, sleeping in the bed and cooking a meal reveals things a quick inspection misses.</p></div>
<h2>What to do when a guest complains about cleanliness</h2>
<p>Even good hosts get the occasional complaint. How you respond matters as much as the problem itself.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reply quickly and politely.</strong> Thank the guest for telling you and avoid sounding defensive.</li>
<li><strong>Fix it during the stay if you can.</strong> Sending someone round to re-clean a bathroom or swap the linen can turn a poor review into a fair one.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for photos.</strong> They help you understand what went wrong and brief your cleaner.</li>
<li><strong>Find the cause.</strong> Was the changeover rushed, a task missing from the list, or a problem like mould that needs a repair?</li>
<li><strong>Respond to public reviews with care.</strong> Keep it short, factual and focused on what you have changed.</li>
